    <bpmn:serviceTask id="postSnapshot" name="POST merged snapshot" camunda:delegateExpression="${restPost}">
      <bpmn:documentation>[REST connector] REST POST - Create a resource: body built from merged sources + pasted OpenAPI schema.
Implementation: delegateExpression ${restPost}
Inputs (leave empty when not needed):
- url: Request URL. Supports ${expressions} and {placeholders} that are filled from params with "x-in": "path". Example: ${apiBaseUrl}/users/{userId}
- sources: Comma separated process variable names (or a JSON literal) deep-merged in order - later wins. Example: currentUser, userChanges
- paramsSchema: Paste the operation's "parameters" list from the service's OpenAPI / Swagger document (in: path | query | header). A parameter is filled from the merged sources, else from the process variable with the same name. Paste it as JSON or YAML (OpenAPI 3.x or Swagger 2.0); "$ref"s must resolve inside the pasted text, so paste the referenced components too, or the whole document plus a top-level "$ref": "#/components/schemas/Name". Add "x-source": "/json/pointer" or "x-expression": "varName" (no dollar-brace wrapper) where your process data is named differently. Long pastes (a whole OpenAPI document) work with history level activity or none; with audit / full history the engine stores every input in history and limits it to 4000 characters.
- bodySchema: Paste the request schema from the service's OpenAPI / Swagger document: a schema object (components.schemas.X), the requestBody, or the whole operation. Builds + validates the body from the merged sources; only declared properties are sent, readOnly ones are left out. Empty = send the merged sources as-is. Paste it as JSON or YAML (OpenAPI 3.x or Swagger 2.0); "$ref"s must resolve inside the pasted text, so paste the referenced components too, or the whole document plus a top-level "$ref": "#/components/schemas/Name". Add "x-source": "/json/pointer" or "x-expression": "varName" (no dollar-brace wrapper) where your process data is named differently. Long pastes (a whole OpenAPI document) work with history level activity or none; with audit / full history the engine stores every input in history and limits it to 4000 characters.
- headers: JSON object, values may use ${expressions}. Example: {"X-Correlation-Id": "${execution.processInstanceId}"}
- responseSchema: Optional. Paste the response schema from the service's OpenAPI / Swagger document: a schema object, a response, or the operation's "responses" (the first 2xx is used). Shapes the response (only declared properties are kept, writeOnly ones dropped) and validates it. Violation -&gt; BPMN error RESPONSE_INVALID. Paste it as JSON or YAML (OpenAPI 3.x or Swagger 2.0); "$ref"s must resolve inside the pasted text, so paste the referenced components too, or the whole document plus a top-level "$ref": "#/components/schemas/Name". Add "x-source": "/json/pointer" or "x-expression": "varName" (no dollar-brace wrapper) where your process data is named differently. Long pastes (a whole OpenAPI document) work with history level activity or none; with audit / full history the engine stores every input in history and limits it to 4000 characters.
- resultVariable: Process variable receiving the response body (stored as JSON).
- statusVariable: Process variable receiving the HTTP status code.
- technicalErrors: exception (default): the task fails - incident, or retried with async before. bpmnError: thrown as BPMN error HTTP_5xx / CONNECTION_FAILED so an error boundary event or error event subprocess can handle it. 4xx (HTTP_4xx) and schema errors (REQUEST_INVALID / RESPONSE_INVALID) are always BPMN errors.
- mockEnabled: Test mode: true = do not send the request, answer with Mock status / Mock response instead (the request is still built and validated, the response schema, error mapping and result variables work as for a real call). false or empty = real call. An expression switches it per instance, e.g. ${execution.getVariable('useMockData') == true}.
- mockStatus: HTTP status the mock answers with (default 200). A 4xx / 5xx tests the error handling, e.g. 404 -&gt; BPMN error HTTP_404.
- mockResponse: Response body the mock answers with: a JSON object / array (or text); empty = no body. ${expressions} inside are evaluated by the engine, so the mock can echo process variables.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements>
        <camunda:inputOutput>
          <camunda:inputParameter name="url">${apiBaseUrl}/customer-snapshots</camunda:inputParameter>
          <camunda:inputParameter name="sources">profile, contact, address, account, loyalty</camunda:inputParameter>
          <camunda:inputParameter name="paramsSchema" />
          <camunda:inputParameter name="bodySchema">$ref: '#/components/schemas/CustomerSnapshot'
components:
  schemas:
    CustomerSnapshot:
      type: object
      required: [id, customerId, name, contact, address, account, loyalty]
      properties:
        id:
          type: string
          readOnly: true
          pattern: '^s-[0-9]+$'
          example: s-17
        customerId:
          type: integer
          format: int64
          x-source: /customer/id
        name:
          type: object
          required: [first, last]
          properties:
            first: {type: string, x-source: /customer/firstName}
            last: {type: string, x-source: /customer/lastName}
        birthDate:
          type: string
          format: date
          nullable: true
          x-source: /customer/birthDate
        contact:
          type: object
          required: [email]
          properties:
            email: {type: string, format: email, x-source: /customer/email}
            phone: {type: string, x-source: /customer/phone}
        address:
          $ref: '#/components/schemas/Address'
        account:
          $ref: '#/components/schemas/Account'
        loyalty:
          $ref: '#/components/schemas/Loyalty'
        snapshotBy:
          type: string
          default: system
          x-expression: execution.getVariable('initiator')
    Address:
      type: object
      required: [city, country]
      properties:
        street: {type: string}
        city: {type: string}
        zip: {type: string}
        country: {type: string, minLength: 2, maxLength: 2, example: IL}
    Account:
      type: object
      required: [status, tier]
      properties:
        status: {type: string, enum: [active, suspended, closed]}
        tier: {type: string, enum: [silver, gold, platinum]}
    Loyalty:
      type: object
      properties:
        points: {type: integer, format: int32}
        level: {type: string}</camunda:inputParameter>
          <camunda:inputParameter name="headers" />
          <camunda:inputParameter name="responseSchema">$ref: '#/components/schemas/CustomerSnapshot'
components:
  schemas:
    CustomerSnapshot:
      type: object
      required: [id, customerId, name, contact, address, account, loyalty]
      properties:
        id:
          type: string
          readOnly: true
          pattern: '^s-[0-9]+$'
          example: s-17
        customerId:
          type: integer
          format: int64
        name:
          type: object
          required: [first, last]
          properties:
            first: {type: string}
            last: {type: string}
        birthDate:
          type: string
          format: date
          nullable: true
        contact:
          type: object
          required: [email]
          properties:
            email: {type: string, format: email}
            phone: {type: string}
        address:
          $ref: '#/components/schemas/Address'
        account:
          $ref: '#/components/schemas/Account'
        loyalty:
          $ref: '#/components/schemas/Loyalty'
        snapshotBy:
          type: string
          default: system
    Address:
      type: object
      required: [city, country]
      properties:
        street: {type: string}
        city: {type: string}
        zip: {type: string}
        country: {type: string, minLength: 2, maxLength: 2, example: IL}
    Account:
      type: object
      required: [status, tier]
      properties:
        status: {type: string, enum: [active, suspended, closed]}
        tier: {type: string, enum: [silver, gold, platinum]}
    Loyalty:
      type: object
      properties:
        points: {type: integer, format: int32}
        level: {type: string}</camunda:inputParameter>
          <camunda:inputParameter name="resultVariable">snapshot</camunda:inputParameter>
          <camunda:inputParameter name="statusVariable">postStatus</camunda:inputParameter>
          <camunda:inputParameter name="technicalErrors">bpmnError</camunda:inputParameter>
          <camunda:inputParameter name="mockEnabled">${execution.getVariable('useMockData') == true}</camunda:inputParameter>
          <camunda:inputParameter name="mockStatus">201</camunda:inputParameter>
